  "id": "ashgrove-house",
  "title": "Ashgrove House",
  "tagline": "A birthday, a bad heart, and a brandy glass nobody else was allowed to touch.",
  "setting": "Ashgrove House, a storm-bound country house on the cliffs north of Calder City",
  "era": "October 1948",
  "intro_briefing": [
    "Saturday, 16 October 1948. The storm came in off the Sound at dusk and has not let up since. Ashgrove House sits on the cliffs twelve miles north of Calder City, at the end of a road that is more mud than gravel tonight, and the telephone line to the house is down.",
    "Edmund Ashgrove, sixty-five today, founder of Ashgrove Shipping, is dead in his study. He had a bad heart and lived on digitalis drops. The police surgeon who rode up with you sniffed his brandy glass and shook his head: the old man's heart did not give out on its own. Somebody gave him enough digitalis to stop a horse.",
    "Three people sat down to his birthday dinner, and the uniforms at the door are keeping all of them here: his daughter Clara Ashgrove, who has run his Pier Seventeen offices for years; her husband Simon Whitlock, a charming man with expensive habits; and Leonard Vance, the family attorney and trustee, who drove down the cliff road through the storm to telephone for you. The cook, Mrs. Dunne, has already told the uniforms what little she saw from the kitchen wing.",
    "Walk the rooms, Detective. Examine whatever looks out of place, question the three of them, and put what you find in front of them. When you can prove who did it, why they did it and how they managed it, make your accusation. You get one chance. Make it count."
  ],
  "victim": {
    "id": "edmund",
    "name": "Edmund Ashgrove",
    "age": 65,
    "description": "Founder of Ashgrove Shipping and its master for forty years; a hard, proud, careful man who wrote everything down. He had a weak heart and was kept going on digitalis drops. Tonight was his sixty-fifth birthday.",
    "cause_of_death": "Heart failure from a massive overdose of digitalis, taken in his after-dinner brandy.",
    "found_in": "study",
    "found_at": "at about 21:55, slumped in his leather armchair by the study fire"
  },
  "solution": {
    "culprit_id": "simon",
    "motive": "Edmund had found out that Simon forged his signature on a forty-thousand-dollar loan guarantee at the Calder Harbor Trust to cover his debts, and he meant to take it to the police on Monday.",
    "method": "While claiming to be on the hall telephone between 21:00 and 21:20, Simon climbed the back stairs to Edmund's bathroom, took the digitalis tincture, emptied it into the engraved brandy glass waiting on the study tray, and buried the empty bottle in the conservatory palm wrapped in his own monogrammed handkerchief.",
    "required_evidence": ["digitalis-bottle", "bank-letter", "telephone-pad"],
    "reveal": "Simon Whitlock was drowning. He owed his brokers more than thirty-eight thousand dollars, and in August he put Edmund's name on a forty-thousand-dollar loan guarantee at the Calder Harbor Trust to keep himself afloat. When the bank wrote asking Edmund to confirm it, the old man knew his own hand, and on the afternoon of his birthday he told Simon he would take the letter to Inspector Harrow of the fraud squad on Monday. At nine o'clock Simon left the drawing room saying he had to ring his broker. Instead he went up the back stairs to Edmund's bathroom, took the bottle of digitalis, and emptied it into the engraved brandy glass that Edmund, as everyone in the house knew, would fill at half past nine. He pushed the empty bottle into the soil of a conservatory palm, wrapped in the first thing that came to hand, his own S.W. handkerchief, and came back with rain in his hair and a story. The story had one flaw: the storm had brought the telephone line down at half past eight, and Edmund, who wrote everything down, had noted it on the hall pad when he tried to reach Harrow himself. The Harbor Trust letter gives the motive, the bottle in the S.W. handkerchief gives the means, and the dead line breaks the only alibi Simon had."
  },
