  "setting": "The Blue Lantern, a jazz club in the Riverside district of Calder City",
  "era": "February 1949",
  "intro_briefing": [
    "February 1949. It has rained on Calder City for three days straight, and tonight it hammers the tin awning of the Blue Lantern, the best jazz room in the Riverside district. It was a packed Saturday show: a full house, a hot band and a singer who can make a stevedore cry into his beer.",
    "At five past midnight the bartender found the owner, Victor Kale, slumped behind the desk in his office behind the stage, shot once in the chest. Somewhere in a room full of horns and laughter a gun went off, and not one soul admits to hearing it.",
    "The uniforms have locked the doors and sent the crowd home. Four people who matter are waiting for you: Kale's manager, Frank Dellow; his singer and former flame, Lena Marsh; her kid brother Danny, who tends the bar; and Kale's attorney, Leonard Vance, who came back through the rain when Frank telephoned him with the news.",
    "Search the main floor, the bar, Kale's office, the dressing room and the alley out back. Question them, show them what you find, and pin it all to the board. When you know who pulled the trigger, make your accusation with proof that will stand up in court, Detective."
  ],
  "victim": {
    "id": "kale",
    "name": "Victor Kale",
    "age": 49,
    "description": "Owner of the Blue Lantern. A former rum-runner who bought his respectability one liquor license at a time: generous with drinks, merciless with debts, and trusting of nobody.",
    "cause_of_death": "A single .38 bullet to the chest, fired at close range. The coroner puts death between 23:30 and 23:50. Nobody in the packed club reported hearing a shot.",
    "found_in": "office",
    "found_at": "at 00:05 by the bartender, Danny Marsh, slumped behind his desk"
  },
  "solution": {
    "culprit_id": "frank",
    "motive": "Victor Kale had found Frank Dellow's notebook proving he had skimmed the door take for a year, and told him that night he would be finished by Monday.",
    "method": "During the long drum solo Frank sent the cashier on her break, took the club revolver from the bar safe whose combination only he and Kale knew, shot Kale in his office under cover of the drums, left by the alley door, dropped the gun in the rain barrel and walked round the block to come back in through the front entrance, soaked.",
    "required_evidence": ["skim-ledger", "safe-revolver", "vance-statement"],
    "reveal": "Frank Dellow had been robbing the Blue Lantern's door for a year, writing the true count in a black notebook and booking a smaller one. That week Victor Kale found the notebook, and at a quarter to ten on Saturday night he told Frank he was through on Monday. Frank arranged the noise himself: he ordered the drummer to play the long solo at 23:35 sharp, sent the cashier off on her break, and while the bar stood empty he opened the safe whose combination only he and Kale knew and took the tagged club revolver. Leonard Vance, sitting in Booth 9 where he had sworn he was not, watched him walk down the back corridor. Under cover of the drums Frank shot Kale across his own desk and tore the drawers apart hunting for the notebook, which Kale had taped behind the painting. He slipped out of the alley door, dropped the gun in the rain barrel, hurried past Lena and Danny Marsh, who were too busy hiding their own faces to see his, and walked round the block in the rain to come back in through the front entrance, soaked, just as the solo ended. The notebook gives him the motive, the safe tag on the revolver gives him the means, and Vance's statement breaks the alibi he built at the front door."
  },
