    "method": "After Pike telephoned her at 22:45, Calloway signed out of the Customs House at 22:50, let herself into the pier through the warehouse wicket with her inspector's key, struck Pike on the temple with the iron ledger press in the archive, pushed him down the stairs to stage a fall, and left with the stamped manifests, missing the carbon copy on his tally board.",
    "required_evidence": ["carbon-manifest", "ledger-press", "signout-log", "tommy-sighting"],
    "reveal": "Harold Pike spent his last evening doing what he had done for thirty-one years: checking the paper against the cargo. At 20:15 he counted the Mary Delacroix's crates against his carbon copy of the manifest, found twelve chalk-marked crates of rye cleared as machine parts, and saw in the customs block the same signature that had cleared five other Ashgrove sailings since November: R. Calloway, No. 114. Leonard Vance came at ten to buy back Pike's dossier on the trust money he had taken; he lost a cufflink at the steel cabinet, left at half past ten and sailed on the 22:40 ferry to Eastbank, with no way back before 23:50. At 22:45 Pike, a decent man to the end, telephoned Ruth Calloway to warn her the commission would have her clearances at nine. She signed out of the Customs House at 22:50, walked ten minutes through the fog, let herself in at the warehouse wicket with her inspector's key and found him in the archive. When he would not give up the folder she hit him with the iron ledger press, sent him down the stairs to make it a fall, set the press back on its shelf with customs violet still on the grip, and walked out under the pier lamp in her rain cape at ten past eleven, where a watchman who should have been on his rounds saw her from the gatehouse window. She burned the stamped manifests; she never knew about the carbon on his tally board. Clara Ashgrove buried a warning to save her company, but she was on the Mariners' Club telephone at eleven. Tommy Reyes drank through his shift and robbed a body that was already cold. The carbon gives the motive, the press the means, the night book breaks her alibi and Tommy's sighting puts her on the pier: only Ruth Calloway had both the reason in the manifests and the hour after 22:50."
