  "suspects": [
    {
      "id": "vance",
      "name": "Leonard Vance",
      "role": "Attorney-at-law of Vance and Pryor, counsel to Ashgrove Shipping and trustee of the Ashgrove Family Trust",
      "age": 55,
      "portrait": "man-glasses",
      "color": "#6b7f99",
      "personality": "Smooth, precise and unfailingly polite, a man who has spent thirty years making other people's trouble sound like paperwork. He never raises his voice; when cornered he grows more courteous, not less. Since February, when his statement put a nightclub manager in the dock, his name has been in the papers more often than he likes. Vain about his reputation, frightened for it this morning, and far too proud to show either.",
      "speech_style": "Measured, lawyerly sentences with qualifications stacked like files: 'strictly speaking', 'as a matter of form', 'I would put it rather differently'. Reaches for a Latin tag when he wants distance (res ipsa loquitur, caveat emptor, de minimis). Calls the detective 'Detective' with a faint smile and answers a question with a question whenever he can.",
      "alibi": "I spent the evening at home in Eastbank with a brief and a glass of port. Strictly speaking, I had not set foot on Pier Seventeen in a month until Miss Ashgrove telephoned me this morning.",
      "public_facts": [
        "Leonard Vance of Vance and Pryor has been Ashgrove Shipping's attorney for twenty years and administers the Ashgrove Family Trust.",
        "Clara Ashgrove telephoned him at dawn; he arrived at the pier at ten past seven, freshly shaved, in a gray homburg."
      ],
      "private_knowledge": [
        "Harold Pike had put together a folder proving that you have been drawing money from the Ashgrove trust since 1945 and passing it through the Blue Lantern's books as band fees. After Victor Kale was shot in February, the club's green ledger went to his estate's accountants, and somehow Pike got photostats of it. He gave you until the first of May to repay it before he went to the Bar Association.",
        "You went to the archive at ten last night to buy the folder back. Pike would not sell at any price and told you the Harbor Commission would hear 'the customs business' at nine in the morning, and you could wait your turn.",
        "When you reached for the folder at the steel cabinet, Pike caught your sleeve. Your cufflink must have come loose then.",
        "When you left at half past ten, Pike was alive and back at his desk with a folder of cargo manifests and the telephone directory open at the Customs House page.",
        "You sailed on the 22:40 Eastbank ferry on the firm's account ticket and were home before half past eleven. Your housekeeper was asleep."
      ],
      "lies": [
